      "name": null,
      "problem": "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x-5k)(x+7k)$ is $x=4.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4,$ we will set our result equal to $4.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(x-5k)(x+7k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=5k$ and ${x}_{2}=-7k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5k+(-7k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k}{2} \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k & =4 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
